# Cache rules, in the order Cloudflare applies them: a later matching rule
# replaces the header a earlier one set, so the broad default goes first and the
# exceptions follow. Deploy prints the headers it actually got back, because
# this ordering is the sort of thing that is easy to assume and worth checking.

# Every page. This rule used to read "/index.html", which matched nothing a
# browser ever asks for — a visitor requests "/", "/streaming", "/hindi", and
# none of those is the literal path index.html. So no HTML page on the site
# carried a revalidation directive from us at all.
#
# That matters more here than on most sites because the filenames below are
# content-hashed and the deploy replaces the whole set: a stale copy of a page,
# held anywhere between the origin and the reader, points at asset URLs that no
# longer exist. Those 404 into the SPA fallback, which answers with HTML, and
# the browser then refuses the stylesheet and the module — leaving the
# prerendered text on a white background, which is exactly the failure being
# chased. Twice-weekly builds and 59 deploys make the window for that wide.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Hashed filenames never change contents, so they can be cached forever.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The calendar is rewritten twice a week; a short TTL means a refresh shows up
# quickly without hammering the origin.
/data/releases.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

# The whole point of this page is to report what the network in front of it is
# doing. A cached copy of it would report on a different day's network.
/diag/*
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
